<!-- Alvik perceive-act body: the robot as the v3 socket body client. The composable
     transport (<AgentSocket>, connecting back to the perceive-act agent's socket
     server) wraps the individual capability routes it serves - `whoami` announces the
     `body` role for the agent's handshake, `apply-heading` maps the chosen Heading onto
     the wheels. The transport and each capability are distinct, visible pieces, not a
     monolithic body element. The agent forwards each `apply-heading` tool call over the
     socket; the same command drives the wgpu fox and this robot.

     apply-heading replies with the step's settle duration and the agent waits it out,
     so the next photo is only taken once the robot has stopped moving. Tune the step
     via DriveStepConfig: duration (how long a heading drives, eg "0.8s"), speed (mm/s)
     and turn_rate (deg/s).

     The transport reconnects on its own with exponential backoff (250ms doubling to a
     10s ceiling), so this scene can be pushed before the agent is up, and survives the
     agent restarting, without being re-pushed.

     Start the perceive-act agent (`ws://<this-host>:8338`, bound to all interfaces),
     push with `beet load templates/alvik/perceive-act-body.bsx` (BEET_REMOTE_URL set
     to the device). Set `url` to the agent's host; omitting it falls back to the
     firmware's BEET_SOCKET_SERVER build env. -->
<AgentSocket url="ws://192.168.86.220:8338">
	<Route path="whoami" {WhoAmi}/>
	<Route path="apply-heading" {(ApplyHeading, DriveStepConfig{duration: "0.8s"})}/>
</AgentSocket>
